# Number-offset comparison and the separate weak-field power limit

The root32 theorem is exact at each fixed model parameter. It must not be
misread as changing root30's leading weak-field power under every fixed
number-sector offset. For the prepared original mark of PR9143,
r_j=kappa O(g^2) at fixed graph. Therefore a fixed gamma in h+gamma N shifts
its selected power by 2 gamma r_j=O(g^2), leaving the leading g->0 power
limit unchanged. Changing that limit through this comparison family requires
an offset that scales with g, typically of order g^-2. Such a scaling is a
separate supplied model choice, not an ordinary scalar energy zero or a
uniform limit theorem proved by root32.

The finite-g count-law nonidentifiability and same-sector input-difference
invariance remain exact. This is a later cross-result scope qualification,
not part of the blind root32 PRE brief, a changed author seal, or evidence
that a physical reservoir supplies the chosen offset.
